out.push(SAMPLE(['Ms Huda asked Mr Attar for the order of the five pieces, and observation, costing, ask, exclusions, occasions, and then one paragraph about what happens if the answer is no, over three pages, with a first page that has no remedy, no ask and nothing about him, which took five drafts and is the only order of the five he tried that a stranger finished. Ms Huda asked why not the ask first, and Mrs Khoury said because an ask before an observation is a request without a reason and the reader\'s first question is why are you telling me this, and four students in the room put the ask first and all four abandoned it within the hour after reading their own second paragraph explaining their first. Mrs Oueidat asked about the occasions, and last of the five, which is the one the room resists, because a proposal that opens with the writer\'s qualifications is a letter of application and the reader has not yet agreed that there is anything to be qualified for. Dr Qabbani asked whether the reader has asked by then, and Mr Attar said he asked it out loud in the meeting before he got to page 3, and why you, which is the whole argument for the order. Mrs Tarabishi asked what is in the last paragraph, and what happens if he says no, and nothing happens: the reconciliation continues, the write-offs continue, and he will not write again before the stock count in February, with no consequence, no warning and no regret, and the reader told him that paragraph was the reason he read page 3 twice. Mrs Khoury said that is because it removes the only thing an unsolicited proposal can threaten, which is the reader\'s peace. Mr Shihabi asked about the length, and three pages, read aloud with a watch at four minutes and ten seconds, which is 21.6. Mr Tayara asked what reading it aloud found, and two things in the middle: a paragraph that repeated the one above it, which came out, and a sentence about Mr Fattal\'s approvals that sounded like a complaint about Mr Fattal. Mrs Rizk asked whether there is a one-page version, and there is, of two hundred and sixty words, and everything in it is in the three-page version. Mrs Ajouz asked which version went, and the three pages, because the reader signs that section\'s time and has met him twice. And Mr Attar said the thing he will take away is that the order of the five pieces is the opposite of the order he wrote them in.']));
out.push(A('⚠ **The last line of the report is the finding of the whole unit.** **The order of the five pieces is the opposite of the order they were written in** — **remedy, occasions, ask, observation** — **because that is the order of the writer\'s own discovery, and a document sent in that order is a set of notes.** **Ask the room, before they look at their drafts, which order they wrote in.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **The ask first** → **four of ten; abandoned within the hour.**   **2** **The occasions first** → **a letter of application.**   **3** ⚠ **A last paragraph that threatens** → **_we cannot afford to let this continue_.**   **4** **A last paragraph that asks for a meeting** → **an undeclared ask.**   **5** ⚠ **A summary containing a new argument** → **it becomes the real document.**   **6** **Never read aloud** → **the repeated paragraph and the complaint sentence are inaudible on paper.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **The hundred and forty-four person-days and the fifteen hours of approvals.** **He says he has not added them together: they are measured in different units, and a single figure would be his rather than the reader\'s** — **and the sentence is thirty words and stays in the document, which is 22.2 surviving into the short version.**'));
out.push(A('**2** ⚠ **That any remedy must keep the eleven genuine short deliveries the hand reconciliation found.** **He asks for the test to be applied to his own report** — **and a writer who hands the reader the test that could defeat him has stopped being a critic of the office.**'));
out.push(A('**3** ⚠ **_The reconciliation continues and I shall not write to you about this again before February._** **It contains no consequence, no warning, no regret and no offer to meet** — **and the reader said that paragraph was the reason he read page 3 twice.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
